  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/1e7f53108e1bbfee39ed331ea4b0d7954b8bdf98310ebe2376008835b908bbbe.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/99d164fc056b13447a549abffd210bb4338e36ec6fe67d148daa5bb68a9a07a5.png)",
